# Database

Odroe starts with a small typed SQL transport instead of pretending one driver
behaves identically on every runtime.

```dart
abstract interface class SqlDatabase {
  Future<List<T>> query<T>(
    BoundSql statement,
    T Function(SqlRow row) decode,
  );

  Future<SqlWriteResult> execute(BoundSql statement);
  Future<List<SqlWriteResult>> atomicWrite(
    List<BoundSql> statements,
  );
}
```

`TransactionalSqlDatabase` is a separate capability. Edge databases that only
support atomic batches do not claim interactive transactions.

## Typed relational queries

```dart
final class Authors extends SqlTable<String> {
  Authors() : super('authors');

  late final id = column<int>('id', sqlInt);
  late final displayName = column<String>('display_name', sqlText);

  @override
  late final projection = SqlProjection.column(displayName);
}

final class Posts extends SqlTable<String> {
  Posts() : super('posts');

  late final id = column<int>('id', sqlInt);
  late final authorId = column<int?>('author_id', nullable(sqlInt));
  late final title = column<String>('title', sqlText);

  @override
  late final projection = SqlProjection.column(title);
}

final posts = Posts();
final authors = Authors();
const sql = SqlQueries(SqlDialect.postgres);

final titles = await sql
    .selectTable(posts, where: posts.id.equals(42))
    .all(database);

final authorName = authors.displayName.optional.as('author_name');
final postsWithAuthors = SqlProjection<({String title, String? authorName})>(
  <SqlSelection<Object?>>[posts.title, authorName],
  (row) => (
    title: posts.title.read(row, 0),
    authorName: authorName.read(row, 1),
  ),
);
final rows = await sql.select(
  from: posts,
  joins: <SqlJoin>[
    SqlJoin.left(
      authors,
      on: posts.authorId.equalsColumn(authors.id),
    ),
  ],
  projection: postsWithAuthors,
).all(database);

await sql.insert(
  posts,
  <SqlAssignment>[posts.title.set('Hello')],
).execute(database);

await sql.updateAll(
  posts,
  <SqlAssignment>[posts.title.set('Archived')],
  confirm: allRows,
).execute(database);
```

The dialect is explicit. `posts.title.set(42)` is an analyzer error.
`updateAll` and `deleteAll` also require the `allRows` confirmation token.
Joined reads qualify every column automatically. For a `LEFT JOIN`, use the
result-only `.optional` selection to decode a non-null schema column as
nullable; it cannot be used for INSERT or UPDATE. Mutations remain single-table.

`SqlQueries` preserves its selected dialect on every generated `BoundSql`.
SQLite and D1 accept `SqlDialect.sqlite`, PostgreSQL accepts
`SqlDialect.postgres`, and MySQL/MariaDB accepts `SqlDialect.mysql`. An explicit
mismatch throws `SqlException` with `SqlErrorCode.unsupported` before that
statement reaches the database.

## BoundSql escape hatch

Use `BoundSql.parts` for SQL outside the typed relational surface.

```dart
final statement = BoundSql.parts(
  <String>['SELECT "title" FROM "posts" WHERE "id" = ', ''],
  <SqlValue>[sqlInt.encode(42)],
  kind: SqlStatementKind.rowReturning,
  dialect: SqlDialect.postgres,
);
final titles = await database.query(
  statement,
  (row) => row.read(0, sqlText),
);
```

Fragments are trusted source. Values are bound. Drivers insert native
placeholders between fragments without parsing or rewriting their contents.
Handwritten `BoundSql.raw` and `BoundSql.parts` statements default to
`dialect: null`: no compatibility is claimed, so drivers continue to accept
them. This does not prove that the SQL is portable. Pin database-specific SQL
with `dialect:` to opt into the same preflight protection as typed queries.

Top-level calls may leave handwritten SQL as `unknown`, but a wrong terminal
may then be detected only after the database executes it. Declare the kind
when it is known. `atomicWrite` and transaction callbacks require an explicit
`write` or `rowReturning` kind, so transaction control and implicitly
committing DDL stay outside framework-managed transactions.

D1 additionally requires `rowReturning` on every `query` call because its raw
result cannot distinguish an empty query from a write. `SqlQueries` supplies
that marker automatically.

## PostgreSQL connections

Keep `PostgresDatabase.open(...)` when one serialized connection is the right
boundary. Server workloads can choose a lazy pool instead:

```dart
final database = PostgresDatabase.pool(
  host: 'localhost',
  database: 'app',
  username: 'app',
  password: secret,
);
```

The default `pool(...)` settings use
`pg.PoolSettings(maxConnectionCount: 4)`. Treat that limit as a database-cost
control and size it for the deployment. Set it explicitly when passing custom
`PoolSettings`; the underlying default for a null limit is one connection.
`poolUrl(...)` has the same one-connection default unless the URL sets
`max_connection_count`. Construction does not open a connection; the first
operation leases one.

Top-level operations may run concurrently. Each `transaction` and
`atomicWrite` operation leases one connection for its complete lifetime.
Concurrency remains bounded by the configured connection limit. Separate
top-level calls are not guaranteed to reuse one session. Temporary tables,
`SET`, and other session-local work are outside the Odroe pool contract. Use
single-connection `open(...)` when that state must span statements, or complete
the whole unit directly inside a caller-owned `pg.Pool.run(...)` callback.

`PostgresDatabase.fromPool(pool)` borrows the pool by default. Closing the
database leaves that pool open. Pass `ownsPool: true` when the database should
close it. Pools created by `pool(...)` and `poolUrl(...)` are owned and released
by `close()`.

## Runtime matrix

| Entrypoint | Runtime | Status | Current boundary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `database_sqlite.dart` | Native SQLite | Verified | Real CRUD, `RETURNING`, batches, and transactions |
| `database_postgres.dart` | Dart VM + PostgreSQL | Verified | Real single-connection, pooled concurrency, and transaction suite |
| `database_d1.dart` | Cloudflare D1 | Preview | Verified with local Wrangler/Workerd; atomic batches, no interactive transaction |
| `database_mysql.dart` | Dart VM + MySQL/MariaDB | Preview | Verified with real MySQL 8.4 and MariaDB 11.8 |
| RPC | Flutter Web | Available | Calls a server-owned database; no browser SQL driver |

The MySQL/MariaDB driver uses one serialized connection. It does not support
nested transactions, multiple result sets, portable `TIME` decoding, or
`RETURNING` through `SqlDialect.mysql`.

MySQL statements without bound values reject `;` before I/O so the driver's
text protocol cannot execute multiple statements. Omit trailing semicolons.
Statements with bound values use the prepared protocol.

D1 has not been verified in a remote Cloudflare deployment.

## Schema and migrations

Keep migrations as reviewable `.sql` files. D1 uses Wrangler migrations.
Server databases use their native migration path. Odroe does not add code
generation or a migration abstraction.
